# main is where we start the pattern.
# BoWaP is basically a spawner that constantly shoots bullets in a circle.
# (8 bullets here) while constantly spinning at an *accelerating* rate.
# So its rotation speed is ever-greater creating a weird effect where
# it alternates between the streams of 8 bullets being aligned, and being unaligned and chaotic
main
    # We fire a bullet that will fire other bullets.
    # This is because we need a constantly rotating spawner.
    fire
        dir abs 0 # fire in an absolute direction of 0. doesn't matter because this spawner doesn't move through space
        speed abs 0 # fire with a speed of 0 i.e. stays still
        bullet shooter # use the "shooter" bullet defined below

# What we use to fire a single bullet in the pattern
fire bwp(direction)
    dir rel direction # we set the firing direction, using the passed in direction argument. This direction is relative to the direction of the bullet that spawned it.
    speed abs 250 # fire with speed of 250
    # the bullet block or the type statement can be omitted if we want to spawn the default bullet
    bullet # we define a bullet to fire...
        type example # ...and set its type to "example", which loads the appropriate scene from the bullet registry.

# We define a bullet.
bullet shooter
    act # This is an action. Essentially, a place where you *do stuff*, such as changing the bullet's angle or speed.
        repeat # loop through the below code forever (there's no expression indicating how many times we want to repeat)
            repeat 8 i # repeat the code below 8 times and save the current iteration count in i
                fire bwp((360/8) * i) # divide a circle in 8 parts and shoot a bullet for the part we're currently in
            chdir # chdir changes the direction, or angle, of the bullet
                # This is where the magic happens.
                # We set the direction to time(), which returns the amount of time passed since the start of the game.
                # 'rel' means we add this angle to the previous angle.
                # If it was 'abs' instead, it would linearly increase with time.
                # But since it's 'rel', the angle increases faster and faster as time passes.
                # Say it starts at angle 10 and 1 frame has passed. We add 1 and get 11.
                # Next frame, We add 2 to 11 and get 13.
                # 13 + 3 = 16
                # 16 + 4 = 20
                # 20 + 5 = 25
                # This quickly spirals out of control.
                # The *4 is just there to make the spinning faster.
                dir rel time()*4 
                over 0 # Change direction over 0 seconds, so instantly.
            waitf 3 # wait 3 frames before shooting again